### Recommended content model

Every subsection should support rich content instead of a single text field.

```

export type ContentBlock =
  | {
      type: "paragraph";
      text: string;
    }
  | {
      type: "heading";
      text: string;
      level: 2 | 3;
    }
  | {
      type: "bullets";
      items: string[];
    }
  | {
      type: "stat";
      label: string;
      value: string;
      context: string;
      sourceIds: string[];
    }
  | {
      type: "chart";
      chartId: string;
      title: string;
      sourceIds: string[];
    }
  | {
      type: "diagram";
      diagramId: string;
      title: string;
      description: string;
    }
  | {
      type: "analysis";
      title: string;
      text: string;
      evidenceStatus:
        | "company-reported"
        | "report-derived"
        | "management-interpretation";
    };

export interface ChapterSection {
  id: string;
  heading: string;
  blocks: ContentBlock[];
  sourceIds: string[];
}

export interface Chapter {
  number: number;
  title: string;
  introduction: string;
  sections: ChapterSection[];
  keyTakeaways: string[];
}

```

This model makes it possible to support long explanations, diagrams, charts, and source attribution within the same section. The renderer should handle each block type consistently across all seven chapters.

## 8. How to build it in Google AI Studio

The most reliable approach is incremental generation with a fixed specification, rather than asking an AI to generate a massive website in a single prompt and hoping every requirement survives.

1. Phase 1 — Generate the foundation

   Create the landing page, design tokens, routing, responsive layout, chapter navigation, and reusable section renderer. Start with representative content rather than filling every page immediately.
2. Phase 2 — Lock the content architecture

   Add the seven chapters and every numbered subsection from the report. Confirm that no heading has been omitted or renamed and that long content can be displayed comfortably.
3. Phase 3 — Populate the research

   Transfer the report's content section by section. Attach citations to individual claims, mark interpretations, and create the corresponding charts and diagrams.
4. Phase 4 — Add the interactive layer

   Implement the timeline, financial explorer, process diagrams, route-network visualization, and presentation mode. Test each interaction rather than accepting decorative controls that do nothing.
5. Phase 5 — Polish and verify

   Check mobile layouts, keyboard navigation, color contrast, source links, numerical accuracy, chart labels, direct chapter URLs, and deployment behavior.

