## 4. Content Depth and Academic Integrity

This is not a conventional short slide deck. It is an extensive website-based presentation.

Each subsection must support detailed explanations, company-specific examples, charts or diagrams where useful, management-theory connections, implications, limitations, and source citations.

Do not impose a fixed number of slides, cards, paragraphs, or screens per subsection. Use as much space as necessary to communicate the complete report accurately.

When the report is supplied, use it as the primary content source. Preserve its framing, terminology, evidence, and chapter structure. Do not silently replace the report with generic online material.

Clearly distinguish:

1. Company-reported facts
2. Facts and arguments presented in the supplied report
3. Management-theory interpretation
4. Additional externally verified research

Never invent internal United Airlines procedures, employee practices, reporting structures, performance measures, quotations, or statistics. If evidence is unavailable, say so or omit the claim.

Do not fabricate source citations, URLs, chart data, or quotations.

